# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules. One canonical origin: the bare apex.
#
# www is a separate origin, so serving the app there would give it its own
# OPFS store and service worker - a user landing on www would see different
# saved state than on the apex. Redirect instead of serving.
#
# That redirect is not here, and cannot be: a `_redirects` source matches a path,
# never a hostname, and Cloudflare lists domain-level redirects as unsupported.
# An `https://www.rom-weaver.com/* https://rom-weaver.com/:splat 301` line lived
# here from v0.7.2 through v0.9.0 and never once fired - Pages skipped it as
# invalid and www served the app directly, on its own origin, the whole time.
#
# It is a zone-level Single Redirect instead (dashboard: rom-weaver.com -> Rules
# -> Redirect Rules, "www to apex"), matching on `http.host`, which is what this
# file cannot do. The www DNS record stays, and stays proxied, or the name would
# not resolve for that rule to catch.
#
# Path-level redirects belong here. A hostname one does not - it will be dropped
# silently.

# Documentation links printed by the CLI, and shipped in crate READMEs, point at
# `/docs/...` here rather than a GitHub blob path. GitHub serves repo files with
# no redirect mechanism at all, so a docs file that moves strands every released
# binary that names it - the reason `docs/cli/reference.md` has to stay behind as
# a stub for versions built before this. A site URL moves with a line in this
# file instead.

